(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-most-likely-45-version-2"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2: Most Likely 45 (Version 2)","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 30-year-old man has used an over-the-counter redness-relief drop every day for a month. His eyes are redder than when he started. What has happened?","choices":["Rebound hyperemia","He has bacterial conjunctivitis","The drops have raised his intraocular pressure","He has developed allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond about two weeks, stopping leaves the eye redder than it started. This is the counseling point most worth carrying out of this topic. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman starting a prostaglandin analog in one eye should be warned about which cosmetic effects?","choices":["Longer lashes and iris color change","Drooping of the eyelid","Yellowing of the sclera","Permanent pupil dilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they matter more when only one eye is treated. Conjunctival hyperemia is common too. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"An 18-month-old needs pressure-lowering therapy. Which class is contraindicated?","choices":["Prostaglandin analogs","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Alpha-2 agonists","Cholinergic agonists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — contraindicated under two years because of central nervous system depression and apnea. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 71-year-old on dorzolamide complains of a persistent bitter taste and stinging on instillation. What do you tell him?","choices":["This suggests an allergy and the drug must stop","This means the drop is contaminated","This indicates systemic absorption and cardiac risk","Both are common with this class"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they are common enough that warning in advance improves adherence. Bitter taste in about a quarter and stinging in about a third. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A patient is being taught to instill an eye drop. What is named as the single most important step?","choices":["Tilting the head back to form a lower lid pocket","Holding the eyes closed for two to three minutes","Washing the hands thoroughly with soap and water","Covering the lacrimal duct after instilling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is marked as the most important step, and it comes before anything else. The dropper must then not touch anything, so it stays sterile. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 47-year-old farm worker develops fungal keratitis after a corneal injury with plant material. Which commercially available ophthalmic agent treats it?","choices":["Trifluridine","Amphotericin B drops","Oral fluconazole alone","Natamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the only commercially available ophthalmic antifungal. It covers Aspergillus, Candida, Cephalosporium, Fusarium and Penicillium. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has been using tobramycin drops for five days and now has worsening pain and a corneal defect. What has happened?","choices":["Rebound hyperemia","Corneal ulceration","Ordinary ocular irritation, common to all ophthalmic antibiotics","A white precipitate from the drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with reactive keratoconjunctivitis. It is one of the few ocular adverse effects specific enough to name a class. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A newborn needs prophylaxis against ophthalmia neonatorum. Which agent?","choices":["Erythromycin ointment","Azithromycin solution","Moxifloxacin solution","Natamycin suspension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the named agent for newborn prophylaxis, and it is inexpensive. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why are younger patients usually intolerant of miotic therapy?","choices":["Systemic bradycardia from muscarinic activation","Visual blurring from induced myopia","Permanent iris color change","Increased airway resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fixed small pupils, induced myopia and visual disturbance are the effects, and a young eye with active accommodation notices them most. That, plus frequent dosing, is why compliance is poor. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the central instruction for a patient given a topical nasal decongestant?","choices":["Do not use it beyond a few days","Use it every day through the allergy season","Use it only at night","Always combine it with a nasal steroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the whole safety message for this drug, and exceeding it is what produces rebound congestion. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient with diabetes is started on a course of prednisone. What should be anticipated?","choices":["Glucose will fall and treatment may need reducing","There is no interaction with antidiabetic medicines","Glucose control will worsen","Insulin requirements will fall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two effects pointing the same way: the steroid raises glucose and blunts the drugs used to lower it. Antidiabetic medicines will work less well. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why must a benzonatate capsule be swallowed whole?","choices":["Chewing destroys the active drug before it works","Chewing causes irritation of the stomach lining","It releases local anesthetic into the mouth","Chewing gives the medicine a bitter taste"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is a local anesthetic, so chewing the capsule releases it in the mouth and throat, numbing the airway a patient needs to protect, and the drug then does not treat the cough. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A child with acute otitis media has a penicillin allergy. Which agents are offered?","choices":["Cefdinir or azithromycin","Ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin","Clindamycin or cefixime","Vancomycin or linezolid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed, though the macrolide is the weaker of the two here because pneumococcal resistance to macrolides runs high. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient on a diuretic is started on dexamethasone. What should be monitored?","choices":["Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Thyroid function tests","Potassium, since both drugs lower it","Prothrombin time and clotting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two drugs pushing the same electrolyte the same way, which is how a patient arrives at a dangerous level without either drug being at fault alone. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why is a patient who has taken a corticosteroid for more than about a week told not to stop abruptly?","choices":["The drug accumulates and must be cleared slowly","Abrupt withdrawal causes fluid retention","The drug loses effect if restarted after a gap","Stopping suddenly produces rebound symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After more than about a week of use, stopping suddenly produces rebound symptoms, so the drug is tapered instead; a short course and a long course are stopped differently. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which patient must not be given dextromethorphan?","choices":["One taking an oral antihistamine","One taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor","One with treated hypertension","One with a productive, chesty cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The restriction runs for two weeks after stopping a monoamine oxidase inhibitor, which is the part most easily missed because the patient has already stopped the drug. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the standard of therapy for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["High-dose amoxicillin alone","Azithromycin","Doxycycline","Amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike otitis media, this one starts with the clavulanate already on board, because beta-lactamase producing Haemophilus influenzae is common here. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which patients should avoid ibuprofen?","choices":["Those with a history of Reye's syndrome","Those with asthma it may worsen","Those taking acetaminophen","Those over sixty-five years old"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is the one most easily forgotten because it is not an obvious consequence of blocking prostaglandin synthesis; previous ulcer or perforation and renal impairment are the other contraindications. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is indicated for heart failure?","choices":["Propranolol","Bisoprolol","Atenolol","Nadolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The three beta blockers used for heart failure are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"In a patient with renal disease whose renal blood flow depends on angiotensin II, what can happen when an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor is started?","choices":["Filtration rate rises sharply","Renal blood flow is unchanged","Potassium falls sharply","Filtration rate drops sharply"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Where renal blood flow depends on angiotensin II, ACE inhibitors can dramatically decrease the glomerular filtration rate, so they are used cautiously and titrated slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A patient in her third trimester cannot take an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor. Is an angiotensin receptor blocker an acceptable substitute?","choices":["Yes, it spares the fetus","Yes, at a reduced dose","No, it is also avoided then","Only if potassium is normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers share the fetal morbidity and mortality and are not given in the second and third trimesters. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"During a nitroprusside infusion, a patient develops trembling, vomiting and convulsions. Sodium thiosulfate is given. Which toxicity does it limit?","choices":["Thiocyanate toxicity","Cyanide toxicity","Lupus syndrome","Hyperkalemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each nitroprusside molecule carries five cyanide groups; cyanide toxicity causes trembling, vomiting and convulsions, and sodium thiosulfate limits it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A patient recently started on prazosin feels lightheaded every time he stands up. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Orthostatic hypotension","Reflex bradycardia","First-dose hyperkalemia","Central sedation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Orthostatic hypotension with postural dizziness is the leading prazosin side effect, alongside headache, drowsiness and lack of energy. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to diltiazem?","choices":["Supraventricular tachycardia","Chronic stable angina","Uncomplicated hypertension","Advanced heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Advanced heart block and hypotension are contraindications; heart failure, liver disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease are relative contraindications. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient takes nadolol. How is it classified?","choices":["Beta-1 selective blocker","Non-selective beta blocker","Alpha-1 and beta blocker","Central alpha-2 agonist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nadolol is one of the first-generation, non-selective agents, with propranolol, penbutolol, pindolol, sotalol and timolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A patient with type 1 diabetes is starting propranolol. What should he be told about hypoglycemia?","choices":["Warning signs may be masked","It will become less frequent","Insulin will no longer be needed","Blood sugar will run high only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers inhibit glycogenolysis, so hypoglycemia lasts longer, and they mask its adrenergic warning symptoms. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has benign prostatic hyperplasia and hypertension. Which single drug treats both?","choices":["Tamsulosin","Clonidine","Terazosin","Amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The other alpha-1 selective agents, terazosin and doxazosin, are used both as antihypertensives and for benign prostatic hyperplasia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which form of niacin is NOT effective as an antilipemic?","choices":["Niacinamide","Nicotinic acid","Extended-release niacin","Immediate-release niacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin is a B-complex vitamin; nicotinic acid is the antilipemic form, and its amide, niacinamide (nicotinamide), is not effective. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does the ACC/AHA (American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association) cholesterol guideline differ from ATP III (Adult Treatment Panel III)?","choices":["It targets an LDL below 100 mg/dL","It starts everyone on high intensity","It makes niacin the first choice","It does not treat to an LDL target"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It starts moderate- or high-intensity statin therapy for the four benefit groups and does not titrate to a specific LDL (low-density lipoprotein) target. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which of these statins is one of the two that can reach high intensity?","choices":["Simvastatin","Pitavastatin","Atorvastatin","Pravastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are the only statins that reach high intensity (50% or greater lowering of LDL, low-density lipoprotein, cholesterol). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which description fits alirocumab?","choices":["Oral bile acid-binding resin","Injectable monoclonal antibody","Oral HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","Oral cholesterol absorption blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are injectable-only monoclonal antibodies, and they are expensive. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A patient on simvastatin develops muscle toxicity. What is required?","choices":["Continue and add a fibrate","Double the dose briefly","Switch to taking it with grapefruit","Discontinue the statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The presence of muscle toxicity requires discontinuing the statin; myopathy occurs in 0.2 to 0.4% with rare rhabdomyolysis. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient on warfarin is started on gemfibrozil. Which interaction is expected?","choices":["Reduced warfarin absorption","Increased anticoagulant effect","Reduced anticoagulant effect","No interaction is expected"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin; they also interact with statins, ezetimibe and bile acid sequestrants. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to niacin?","choices":["Chronic liver disease","Peptic ulcer disease","History of symptomatic gout","Diabetes with glucose intolerance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic liver disease is the absolute contraindication; peptic ulcer disease, symptomatic gout, significant hyperuricemia and diabetes are relative ones. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Why are bile acid sequestrants approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy?","choices":["They are cleared quickly by the kidney","They are not absorbed from the gut","They have no effect on other drugs","They have no gastrointestinal effects"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because resins stay in the gastrointestinal tract, systemic side effects are limited; they are called the safest drug for that reason. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"In which patient is a fibrate contraindicated?","choices":["One with triglycerides over 1000 mg/dL","One with a low HDL cholesterol","One with existing gallbladder disease","One with well-controlled diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrate contraindications are pregnancy, severe hepatic or renal dysfunction, and existing gallbladder disease; cholelithiasis is also an adverse effect. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with stable angina has a history of hives and wheezing after taking aspirin. Which drug should replace aspirin to prevent acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Isosorbide mononitrate","Clopidogrel","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is as effective as aspirin for secondary prevention and is recommended when a patient is allergic to aspirin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with an acute coronary syndrome still has chest pain after aspirin. Blood pressure is 130/82 mmHg and he takes no phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor. Which drug relieves his pain but does not improve survival?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Clopidogrel","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates give chest pain relief with no mortality benefit, starting sublingually and moving to an intravenous infusion in hospital. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with ST-elevation myocardial infarction still rates his pain 9 out of 10 after repeated sublingual nitroglycerin. Blood pressure is 124/76 mmHg. Which drug is used for chest pain unresponsive to nitrates?","choices":["Metoprolol","Clopidogrel","Enoxaparin","Morphine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the opioid analgesic used for pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation infarction, though its use is controversial. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with stable angina has type 2 diabetes mellitus and a reduced ejection fraction of 35 percent. He takes aspirin and metoprolol. Which added drug may slow progression of his coronary disease?","choices":["An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","A long-acting nitrate","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is used in coronary disease with diabetes or left ventricular systolic dysfunction and may prevent disease progression, though it does not relieve angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has three hours of chest pressure. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression, and the cardiac biomarker is elevated. He has no bleeding history. Which therapy is not recommended for him?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Reteplase","Enoxaparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolytics are not recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome, because the bleeding risk outweighs the benefit. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with hypertension and stable angina has a blood pressure of 154/94 mmHg and a heart rate of 82 beats per minute. His clinician plans a calcium channel blocker. Which calcium channel blocker should be avoided?","choices":["Long-acting nifedipine","Short-acting nifedipine","Amlodipine","Felodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Its abrupt vasodilation causes hypotension and reflex tachycardia, which can worsen ischemia, so short-acting agents are avoided in angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with stable angina opened his bottle of sublingual nitroglycerin tablets eight months ago. He keeps it in the original container in a bedroom drawer and has used it twice. In the traditional counseling, what should he be told about the tablets?","choices":["Keep using them until the bottle is empty","Move them to a weekly pill organizer","Refrigerate them to extend their life","Replace them with a fresh supply"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The traditional counseling is to replace opened tablets about every 3 to 6 months so that a potent tablet is ready in an emergency. Current labeling ties expiry to the printed date when the tablets stay in the tightly closed original glass bottle. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with known angina develops chest pressure at rest. He sits down and places one nitroglycerin tablet under his tongue. Five minutes later the pain is unchanged. What should he do next?","choices":["Call emergency services","Wait an hour to see if rest helps","Swallow a long-acting nitrate tablet","Take his beta blocker early"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chest pain unrelieved five minutes after the first nitroglycerin dose is treated as a possible acute coronary syndrome, so emergency help is called. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"When left ventricular function is decreased and a beta blocker cannot be used, which calcium channel blocker is the alternative?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Nifedipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are first line with decreased left ventricular function; amlodipine is the calcium channel blocker alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
